<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/"><channel><title>Technical Writing on James Robb</title><link>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/</link><description>Recent content in Technical Writing on James Robb</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><managingEditor>me@jamesrobb.ca (James Robb)</managingEditor><webMaster>me@jamesrobb.ca (James Robb)</webMaster><copyright>© 2026 James Robb</copyright><lastBuildDate>Tue, 01 Dec 2020 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Master's Thesis</title><link>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/masters_thesis/</link><pubDate>Tue, 01 Dec 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>me@jamesrobb.ca (James Robb)</author><guid>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/masters_thesis/</guid><description/><content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;My master&amp;rsquo;s thesis is based on my paper &lt;a
href="https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/nested_but_separate/"&gt;Nested, but Separate: Isolating Unrelated Critical Sections in Real-Time Nested Locking&lt;/a&gt;. It provides a much more thorough background on real-time locking to make the material accessible to a wider computer science audience.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="msc_robb_2020.pdf"&gt;&lt;span class="iconwrapper"&gt;
&lt;span class="relative inline-block align-text-bottom icon"&gt;&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512"&gt;&lt;path fill="currentColor" d="M48 64C21.5 64 0 85.5 0 112c0 15.1 7.1 29.3 19.2 38.4L236.8 313.6c11.4 8.5 27 8.5 38.4 0L492.8 150.4c12.1-9.1 19.2-23.3 19.2-38.4c0-26.5-21.5-48-48-48H48zM0 176V384c0 35.3 28.7 64 64 64H448c35.3 0 64-28.7 64-64V176L294.4 339.2c-22.8 17.1-54 17.1-76.8 0L0 176z"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt; Master&amp;rsquo;s Thesis PDF&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</content:encoded></item><item><title>Nested, but Separate: Isolating Unrelated Critical Sections in Real-Time Nested Locking</title><link>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/nested_but_separate/</link><pubDate>Fri, 01 May 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>me@jamesrobb.ca (James Robb)</author><guid>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/nested_but_separate/</guid><description/><content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;Björn Brandenburg and I introduced a novel fined-grained nested-locking protocol for real-time systems under clustered scheduling. Our protocol provides temporal isolation for latency-sensitive tasks that access (possibly) nested resources while remaining asymptotically optimal. The paper is published in the proceedings of the 32nd Euromicro Conference on Real-Time Systems (ECRTS).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="ecrts2020.pdf"&gt;&lt;span class="iconwrapper"&gt;
&lt;span class="relative inline-block align-text-bottom icon"&gt;&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512"&gt;&lt;path fill="currentColor" d="M48 64C21.5 64 0 85.5 0 112c0 15.1 7.1 29.3 19.2 38.4L236.8 313.6c11.4 8.5 27 8.5 38.4 0L492.8 150.4c12.1-9.1 19.2-23.3 19.2-38.4c0-26.5-21.5-48-48-48H48zM0 176V384c0 35.3 28.7 64 64 64H448c35.3 0 64-28.7 64-64V176L294.4 339.2c-22.8 17.1-54 17.1-76.8 0L0 176z"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt; ECRTS Paper PDF&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="ecrts2020_extended.pdf"&gt;&lt;span class="iconwrapper"&gt;
&lt;span class="relative inline-block align-text-bottom icon"&gt;&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512"&gt;&lt;path fill="currentColor" d="M48 64C21.5 64 0 85.5 0 112c0 15.1 7.1 29.3 19.2 38.4L236.8 313.6c11.4 8.5 27 8.5 38.4 0L492.8 150.4c12.1-9.1 19.2-23.3 19.2-38.4c0-26.5-21.5-48-48-48H48zM0 176V384c0 35.3 28.7 64 64 64H448c35.3 0 64-28.7 64-64V176L294.4 339.2c-22.8 17.1-54 17.1-76.8 0L0 176z"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt; Extended Technical Report PDF&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</content:encoded></item><item><title>Bachelor's Thesis</title><link>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/bachelors_thesis/</link><pubDate>Wed, 13 Jun 2018 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>me@jamesrobb.ca (James Robb)</author><guid>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/bachelors_thesis/</guid><description/><content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;Sigurður Helgason and I explore in our bachelor&amp;rsquo;s thesis, &lt;em&gt;Identifying Combinatorial Structures for Binary Strings and Set Partitions&lt;/em&gt;, how to build combinatorial specifications for avoidance classes of binary strings and set partitions. Building upon the work of Bean et al. and the CombSpecSearcher system, we show that we can find a combinatorial specification for any avoidance class of binary strings, and combinatorial specifications for many avoidance classes of set partitions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I think even in tech and computer science it&amp;rsquo;s unlikely most people know what a combinatorial specification or avoidance class is, so here&amp;rsquo;s a quick motivating example with binary strings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s say you wanted to know how many binary strings of length 10 &lt;em&gt;do not&lt;/em&gt; contain the substring &amp;lsquo;101&amp;rsquo;. This would be easy enough to brute force, but what if you then wanted to know the same thing for binary strings of length 9999? This would be considerably more difficult to brute force. In my thesis I show how to count the number of binary strings of any length that &lt;em&gt;avoid&lt;/em&gt; (i.e., do not contain) some given substring. I also explore the same idea applied to set partitions (with plenty of cool &lt;a
href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PGF/TikZ"
target="_blank"
&gt;tikz&lt;/a&gt; graphics!), but you&amp;rsquo;ll have to read the thesis to see how.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="bsc_robb_2018.pdf"&gt;&lt;span class="iconwrapper"&gt;
&lt;span class="relative inline-block align-text-bottom icon"&gt;&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 512 512"&gt;&lt;path fill="currentColor" d="M48 64C21.5 64 0 85.5 0 112c0 15.1 7.1 29.3 19.2 38.4L236.8 313.6c11.4 8.5 27 8.5 38.4 0L492.8 150.4c12.1-9.1 19.2-23.3 19.2-38.4c0-26.5-21.5-48-48-48H48zM0 176V384c0 35.3 28.7 64 64 64H448c35.3 0 64-28.7 64-64V176L294.4 339.2c-22.8 17.1-54 17.1-76.8 0L0 176z"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt; Bachelor&amp;rsquo;s Thesis PDF&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</content:encoded></item><item><title>Optical Character Recognition of Cyrillic Characters Using a Convolutional Neural Network</title><link>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/dragnet/</link><pubDate>Mon, 27 Mar 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>me@jamesrobb.ca (James Robb)</author><guid>https://jamesrobb.ca/technical_writing/dragnet/</guid><description/><content:encoded>&lt;p&gt;I created a convolutional neural network and a set of complementary utilities for optical character recognition with two classmates in my Introduction to Artificial Intelligence course at Reykjavik University. In particular, I focused on reconstructing water damaged documents written in the Ukrainian language. We used Tensorflow for the neural network and the &lt;a href="https://github.com/jamesrobb/dragnet"&gt;code is published on GitHub &lt;span class="iconwrapper"&gt;
